<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Xieng Thong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1559–1560</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Xieng Thong is a Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, built in 1559–1560 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> of Lan Xang. With its sweeping tiered roofs it is the finest example of the Luang Prabang style, and it remained a centre of royal ceremony for the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-xieng-thong.jpg" alt="Wat Xieng Thong" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basile Morin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Wat_Xieng_Thong_-_Luang_Prabang_-_Laos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
